{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785183895,"nonce":"5K7zo+hv0xDPESponk6HgGFsDGI=","title":"ADR-0062 s4: Workspace Export lifecycle — the export path IS the capability","message":"The per-Step Export: the service mounts `overlayfs` with a Snapshot Farm as lower and a per-Step directory as upper, and hands out an address for it. Then settles it back into the CAS via the change set, and reaps it.\n\n**The fence is the hard part.** NFS authenticates with `AUTH_SYS` — the client asserts a uid and the server believes it — so per-Step isolation cannot come from the protocol. It must come from the export path being an unguessable 256-bit secret, TTL'd to the Step deadline, **pinned to the first client that mounts it**, and revoked when the Attempt settles. That is structurally the same capability the existing per-Pod HMAC workspace token is (`mint_workspace_token`, `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:397`), delivered through a different channel.\n\nNo NFS server yet — this slice is the lifecycle and the capability, with the Export as a directory plus a capability record.\n\n\n- [ ] prepare → returns an unguessable path + TTL; settle → returns the new snapshot root and content identity; revoke/reap removes the directory and the record\n- [ ] A second distinct client is refused after first-mount pinning\n- [ ] An expired capability is refused, and expiry is tied to the Step deadline rather than a fixed constant\n- [ ] Settling uses the s2 change set, so an untouched Step re-hashes nothing and reproduces its input's content identity\n- [ ] Capability values never appear in logs\n- [ ] Reaping is idempotent and a leaked Export is detectable\n\n\n- 873d301 (s1, the Farm is the lower layer)\n- s2 (the change set is how an Export settles)","files":null}]}